          {
            "text": "I wrote `{\"invariant_exists\": true}` to the answer file. I didn't run anything: this environment can't execute code. I also didn't open the test file or `adam.py`; the answer comes from how Adam's momentum update works in general, so it rests on one assumption.\n\n**Why it holds:** the momentum slot `m` starts at zero and changes by `m \u2190 \u03b2\u2081\u00b7m + (1\u2212\u03b2\u2081)\u00b7g` each step, with 0 \u2264 \u03b2\u2081 < 1. That makes every value of `m` a weighted average of zero and the gradients seen so far, with weights that add up to at most 1. So `|m|` can never be larger than the biggest `|g|` seen for that coordinate. Setting `amsgrad=False` only changes the second-moment slot, not `m`.\n\n**The assumption:** that Keras's `update_step` implements exactly this update, probably written as `m += (g \u2212 m)\u00b7(1\u2212\u03b2\u2081)`, which is the same thing. I didn't open `adam.py` to confirm this, and I didn't check whether any of the 12 tests use unusual \u03b2\u2081 values or gradients.\n\n**One risk:** float32 rounding could, in theory, push `m` a tiny bit past the largest gradient, for example when the same gradient is repeated many times. I judged that unlikely enough not to change the answer.",
